# Droplet Edge Platform — Environment Variable Reference
# -------------------------------------------------------
# This file is documentation only — setup.sh generates .env directly
# via heredoc (no template dependency). Keep as a reference for developers.
#
# To provision a device: ./scripts/setup.sh
# To regenerate secrets: ./scripts/setup.sh --regenerate-env

# --- PostgreSQL ---
POSTGRES_USER=droplet
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=droplet
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://droplet:change-me@db:5432/droplet?sslmode=require

# --- Redis ---
# WARP-234: REDIS_PASSWORD authenticates the ping-only `default` ACL user
# (health probes / verification harness). Services use their own ACL
# identities below via the per-service rediss:// URLs in docker-compose.yml.
REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me
REDIS_URL=rediss://:change-me@cache:6380
REDIS_PASSWORD_ORCHESTRATOR=change-me
REDIS_PASSWORD_AI_GATEWAY=change-me
REDIS_PASSWORD_MCP=change-me

# --- MQTT (WARP-235: mTLS — identity is the per-service client cert CN) ---
# No shared password. Each client presents its bundle from
# data/secrets/service-tls/<service>/ (issued by the WARP-236 internal CA);
# per-CN topic grants live in docker/mosquitto.acl.
# See docs/security/internal-mtls.md.
MQTT_BROKER=mqtts://broker:8883
# Host-network services (camera-discovery) dial the loopback publish.
MQTT_BROKER_LOCAL=mqtts://localhost:8883

# --- Internal service-to-service mTLS (WARP-236 plumbing, WARP-1061 wiring) ---
# 0 (default) = internal HTTP/gRPC hops speak plaintext exactly as before.
# 1 = every first-party listener (orchestrator :3000, the FastAPI/uvicorn
#     services, ai-gateway gRPC :50051, matter-controller :8083, nginx→upstream
#     legs) serves TLS and REQUIRES a CA-signed client cert; every first-party
#     client presents its data/secrets/service-tls/<service>/ bundle.
# Flip only AFTER ./scripts/setup.sh has issued the bundles, then recreate the
# stack (docker compose up -d --force-recreate) and re-run the host installers
# (install-device-bridge.sh, single-box.sh path) so host-side callers pick up
# their client certs. MQTT is scheme-gated (mqtts://) independent of this knob.
# See docs/security/internal-mtls.md for the enforcement matrix + exemptions.
DROPLET_INTERNAL_TLS=0

# --- Nextcloud ---
NEXTCLOUD_ADMIN_USER=admin
NEXTCLOUD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
NEXTCLOUD_URL=http://nextcloud:80
# Optional: file-indexer's connection for Nextcloud file-id lookups
# (WARP-1327). Defaults to DATABASE_URL with only the db name swapped to
# `nextcloud`; a custom role here needs the WARP-1328 oc_* read grants.
#NEXTCLOUD_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Document server (WARP-882 / WARP-1686 — in-browser viewing/editing) ---
# In-browser Office viewing/editing via a WOPI document engine behind the
# `docs` compose profile, integrated through a Nextcloud connector app. The
# engine is ENGINE-SELECTABLE (ADR-034): every seam is WOPI, so switching is
# config, not code.
#
# DEFAULT-ON on the 32 GB box; drop on ≤8 GB boxes. The engine adds a ~2 GB
# always-on image, which a 32 GB single-box absorbs comfortably, so
# scripts/setup.sh ships it ON there: it RAM-gates the `docs` profile +
# DOCS_ENABLED in scripts/lib/single-box.sh (default-ON above the small-box
# threshold, dropped on a ≤8 GB box to reclaim the ~2 GB). With it off, the
# dashboard renders the "Edit" affordance as unavailable, not dead.
#
# LICENSE (ADR-034): the default engine is Collabora CODE — LibreOffice
# technology, MPLv2 core, NO licensing fee, which is exactly why it is the
# default (OnlyOffice CE is AGPLv3 and shipping it at GA requires a paid
# OnlyOffice OEM/commercial license — that gate now applies ONLY to boxes an
# operator flips to DOCS_ENGINE=onlyoffice). No license enforcement in code.
#
# DOCS_ENABLED — EXPLICIT master switch (NOT derived from the URL). Default 1
#   (on; the 32 GB box ships the engine). Set to 0 AND drop `docs` from
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES on a ≤8 GB box to reclaim the ~2 GB engine.
DOCS_ENABLED=1
# DOCS_ENGINE — which engine runs behind the `docs` profile. One of:
#   collabora  (default) Collabora CODE (LibreOffice; no fee). Connector app:
#              `richdocuments` (Nextcloud Office). coolwsd listens on :9980
#              under net.service_root=/docs.
#   onlyoffice OnlyOffice Document Server CE (AGPLv3; OEM license before GA).
#              Connector app: `onlyoffice`. Listens on :80 at its root.
# DOCS_ENGINE_IMAGE and DOCS_INTERNAL_URL MUST track this knob — setup.sh
# (scripts/lib/single-box.sh) writes the trio together so they never diverge.
DOCS_ENGINE=collabora
# DOCS_ENGINE_IMAGE — the engine container image; tracks DOCS_ENGINE:
#   collabora  → collabora/code:26.04.2.4.1
#   onlyoffice → onlyoffice/documentserver:8.2
DOCS_ENGINE_IMAGE=collabora/code:26.04.2.4.1
# DOCS_INTERNAL_URL — compose-network base URL the orchestrator probes for the
#   engine's health; tracks DOCS_ENGINE:
#   collabora  → http://docserver:9980/docs   (coolwsd port + service_root)
#   onlyoffice → http://docserver             (DS serves :80 at its root)
DOCS_INTERNAL_URL=http://docserver:9980/docs
# Public path the gateway fronts the engine on (nginx `location /docs/`).
DOCS_EDITOR_PUBLIC_PATH=/docs/
# Per-session editor access-token TTL (seconds). The dashboard refreshes before
# expiry. 1800 = 30 min.
DOCS_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=1800
# Engine container resource ceilings (ADR-021). docserver is ~2 GB, additive on
# top of the default profile. Raise on a larger box.
DOCS_MEM_LIMIT=2g
DOCS_CPUS=2.0
DOCS_PIDS_LIMIT=1024
# ONLYOFFICE_JWT_SECRET — shared HS256 secret the OnlyOffice engine, the
#   Nextcloud `onlyoffice` connector, AND the orchestrator's editor session
#   tokens all sign/verify with. Still REQUIRED under DOCS_ENGINE=collabora:
#   the orchestrator signs its short-lived editor-session tokens with it
#   (Collabora itself needs no shared secret — its trust model is the
#   aliasgroup WOPI-host allowlist + proof keys). GENERATED per-device by
#   scripts/setup.sh (openssl rand -hex 32) into .env; lives ONLY there (never
#   tracked), never logged. Leave this EMPTY in the example — NEVER a literal
#   like `change-me`, which would be a forgeable HS256 secret on a default box.
#   Empty ⇒ the orchestrator treats the doc-server as unavailable (fail-safe:
#   no document-access JWTs with a default secret).
ONLYOFFICE_JWT_SECRET=
# Whether the OnlyOffice engine enforces the JWT (leave on; matched by the
# connector; ignored by Collabora).
ONLYOFFICE_JWT_ENABLED=true

# --- AI Gateway ---
AI_GATEWAY_URL=http://ai-gateway:8000
# Canonical chat path: direct to Ollama on port 11434, no /proxy suffix.
# Pick the variant that matches your deployment:
#   • Single-box (Ollama in the bundled `droplet-ollama` container on
#     the compose default network) — `http://droplet-ollama:11434`.
#     This is what setup.sh writes by default; see scripts/lib/secrets.sh.
#   • Local Docker Desktop dev (Ollama on the host at 127.0.0.1:11434) —
#     `http://host.docker.internal:11434` (the default below). Requires
#     `extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"]` on whichever
#     service is calling — currently only orchestrator + mcp-server have
#     it; ai-gateway does NOT, so the host-Ollama path won't work for
#     chat until that mapping is added.
#   • Multi-box production (separate inference host on the LAN) — point at
#     the inference host's static IP, e.g. `http://192.168.50.197:11434`.
#     The legacy `http://inference-engine.local:11434` looks similar but
#     does NOT resolve from inside Docker containers (mDNS doesn't cross
#     the bridge), so don't use it.
# ollama-manager's /proxy on :8002 still exists as an OPT-IN observability
# layer (tool-call JSON repair, circuit breaker, model /health.limits
# contract) — point OLLAMA_URL there if you want those signals, but
# note the manager's 120s upstream read timeout will bite heavy agent-loop
# prompts on CPU. See ADR-004 in droplet-local-LLM.
OLLAMA_URL=http://dmr:12434
# The model-lifecycle manager's /health endpoint (NOT Ollama's — :11434 has no
# /health). The ai-gateway reads the appliance's queue limits (num_parallel,
# max_queue) from here to size outbound concurrency, and /ai/readiness reflects
# it. This is DECOUPLED from the chat OLLAMA_URL above (XR-05): leave it unset
# on the single-box / direct-:11434 path (no manager deployed) — the gateway
# then runs with sane default limits and does NOT probe a non-existent /health.
# Set it only when the manager (:8002) is deployed, e.g.:
#   INFERENCE_MANAGER_URL=http://inference-manager:8002
# (If OLLAMA_URL itself ends in /proxy, the manager root is derived from it.)
#
# NOTE the hostname. This example previously read `droplet-ollama-manager`,
# which has NEVER resolved on any network: droplet-local-LLM's compose file has
# never set `container_name`, so compose names the container
# `<project>-<service>-1` and the only DNS name it answers to is its SERVICE
# key. `inference-manager` is that key (and `ollama-manager` still resolves via
# an explicit alias through the deprecation window — see WARP-1748). Copying
# the old example produced a DNS failure, then the gateway skipping the probe
# and running on default limits, with nothing obviously wrong in the logs.
#
# WARP-1748 — this was `OLLAMA_MANAGER_URL`. The service became a runtime-
# AGNOSTIC model-lifecycle manager in WARP-1743 (INFERENCE_RUNTIME selects the
# backend; `ollama` is only the default), so the OLLAMA_ prefix named an
# implementation detail rather than the contract. NOT related to OLLAMA_URL
# above, which stays as-is — that one is the chat endpoint.
# INFERENCE_MANAGER_URL=
#
# DEPRECATED — `OLLAMA_MANAGER_URL` is still read as a fallback so the .env on
# an already-deployed box keeps working, and the ai-gateway logs a warning
# naming this file when it falls back. INFERENCE_MANAGER_URL wins when both are
# set. Rename your line rather than adding a second one; a future ticket removes
# the fallback once every provisioned box has migrated (same lifecycle as
# ADR-011's JETSON_OLLAMA_URL → OLLAMA_URL rename).
#
# Deliberately NOT left here as a commented-out `OLLAMA_MANAGER_URL=` line. A
# commented key in this file is a template — it reads as an invitation, and a
# new deployment copying it would opt itself onto the deprecated name on day
# one, extending a window whose whole purpose is to close. The fallback exists
# for boxes that ALREADY have it, not for boxes being provisioned now.
# Read timeout (seconds) for Ollama HTTP calls. Cold-loading a 7-8B model with
# partial GPU offload can take 60-90s on a low-power inference host. Default 300 is a
# safe upper bound; bump higher only if you see 499s on first chat after boot.
OLLAMA_READ_TIMEOUT=300
# Context window for the bundled single-box Ollama (WARP-854). Ollama's
# baked-in default is 4096 tokens, which the owner-role agent loop's tool
# schemas alone overflow — the symptom is an INSTANT empty chat answer
# (finish_reason=length, zero output tokens). The compose default is 16384;
# raise it for long documents/history if your GPU has KV-cache headroom,
# lower it on small-VRAM boards. Requires --force-recreate of `ollama`.
# OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=16384
# Window-experiment levers (agent-budgets spec §5) — leave unset until the
# spec §6 measurement protocol picks values. q8_0 KV cache roughly halves KV
# VRAM; flash attention is its prerequisite. Both apply to the bundled
# `ollama` service only.
# OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1
# OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0
# --- Docker Model Runner (dark profile `dmr` — WARP-1772, epic WARP-1740) ---
# Second ADR-036 runtime backend. NOT started by default and NOT serving
# anything: it activates only when an operator adds `dmr` to COMPOSE_PROFILES,
# and the serving flip itself is a separate, hard-gated change (WARP-1749
# preconditions: lab soak + size_vram product decision + explicit human
# sign-off). See docs/dmr-single-box.md before touching any of these.
# DMR_IMAGE=docker/model-runner:v1.2.6-rocm
# DMR_PORT=12434
# DMR_CONTEXT_LENGTH=16384
# DMR_MEM_LIMIT=4g
# DMR_CPUS=4.0
# DMR_PIDS_LIMIT=2048
# WARP-1870: DMR is now the DEFAULT for a freshly provisioned box —
# scripts/lib/secrets.sh writes INFERENCE_RUNTIME and LLM_MODEL directly, and
# scripts/lib/single-box.sh derives the compose profile and both URLs from the
# runtime. These are no longer flip-only values; they ship active.
#
# Never guess LLM_MODEL under DMR: it must be the EXACT id `GET /api/tags`
# reports (registry-qualified). The readiness check compares raw strings, so
# any other spelling of the same model reads as absent and re-pulls ~13.8 GB
# on EVERY orchestrator boot. An EMPTY value is worse — nothing to acquire at
# all, i.e. a dead appliance out of the crate.
#
# To provision an Ollama box instead: INFERENCE_RUNTIME=ollama ./scripts/setup.sh
# (that also swaps the `dmr` compose profile for `ollama` — the two are
# mutually exclusive, see COMPOSE_PROFILES below).
#
# An ALREADY-DEPLOYED box is never flipped by a setup re-run: migrate_env
# backfills a missing INFERENCE_RUNTIME as `ollama`, which is what it has
# been serving all along.
INFERENCE_RUNTIME=dmr
LLM_MODEL=docker.io/ai/gpt-oss:20B-F16
RAGAS_OLLAMA_URL=http://dmr:12434/v1
# Agent loop step limit (agent-budgets spec §1) — the default the loop uses
# when a caller doesn't set max_iter, and the hard cap no caller can exceed.
# Default 10 / cap 10, set by the 2026-07-21 staging step-sweep measurement
# (staging-seed/eval/findings-2026-07-21-tuning.md: 23/36 twice-confirmed at
# 10 vs 21 at the old default 5, with no typical-turn latency cost).
# AGENT_MAX_ITER_DEFAULT=10
# AGENT_MAX_ITER_CAP=10
# WARP-1479 — include a 500-char excerpt of the RAW model completion in the
# `blank_final_answer` log line when a turn produces no visible answer. The
# counts and the cause label are logged either way; only the excerpt is
# gated, because raw completion text can quote the user's own documents.
# Turn on for a debugging window, then off again.
# AGENT_BLANK_TURN_DEBUG=0
# Relevance-based tool advertisement (agent-budgets spec §3) — "domains"
# narrows each turn's advertised tools to a core set + keyword-matched
# domains (plus domains already used in the conversation) instead of the full
# chat scope; self-heals a wrong guess at the cost of one iteration.
#
# WARP-1921: ships "domains". The §6 phase-3 cell scored 24/36 — best of the
# 2026-07-21 sweep, with zero self-heals and zero degradation drops — and was
# held only by the cross-turn continuity gap, now closed. Measured: ~12.7K
# tokens of tool schemas on EVERY turn drops to ~2.6K on a camera turn.
#
# Set "off" to roll back to full-pool advertisement. Note that `off` no longer
# fits the 16K window alongside the full fixed blocks (the pool passed the
# ceiling during WARP-1893), so it leans on the runtime degradeToFit gate —
# it is a diagnostic/rollback mode, not a supported steady state.
# TOOL_SELECTION_MODE=domains
# Model the orchestrator uses for its INTERNAL LLM calls (HyDE / multi-query
# rewrites, retrieval eval, email analysis) — distinct from the model the user
# picks in the chat UI. Defaults to LLM_MODEL (the model the box actually
# pulls; single-box.sh writes it to .env), so normally leave this unset.
# DEFAULT_MODEL=
# Reasoning effort applied to the always-on voice assistant (_service:voice)
# when it doesn't request one itself (WARP-1442). Voice replies are one short
# spoken sentence, so the gpt-oss reasoning channel is wasted decode latency;
# "low" trims it server-side (voice-io sends nothing new). Values: low | medium
# | high; anything else falls back to "low". Only affects the voice principal —
# the dashboard and other callers are unchanged unless they set the field
# explicitly per request. Read by the orchestrator.
# VOICE_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# --- Image vision (chat) ---
# Preferred LOCAL vision model for image attachments. When the user attaches an
# image and their selected chat model can't see, the orchestrator auto-routes
# that turn to this model (and model-readiness pulls it at startup, like
# LLM_MODEL). Leave unset for no local vision — cloud vision still works by
# selecting a cloud vision model (gpt-4o / Claude) explicitly. Read by the
# orchestrator.
# VISION_MODEL=llama3.2-vision:11b
# Max images re-sent per chat request (most-recent-first), bounding token cost
# on a small local context window. Orchestrator; default 3, range 1-8.
# VISION_MAX_IMAGES=3
# Longest-side pixel cap for the normalized vision.jpg render the file-indexer
# writes per image (downscaled, EXIF-rotated JPEG). Read by file-indexer;
# default 1024.
# VISION_RENDER_MAX_PX=1024
# --- Outbound email reconcile (WARP-890) ---
# Grace window (ms) before an outbound email draft stranded in `sending` is
# reconciled to `failed`. A draft is claimed queued->sending before the SMTP
# send; if its terminal status callback never lands (email-indexer crash / lost
# PATCH) it would otherwise strand. Both the orchestrator's stale-sending cron
# (every 5 min) and the email-indexer's outbound tick sweep `sending` rows whose
# claimedAt is older than this window to `failed` (never re-queued). Read by the
# orchestrator; default 600000 (10 min). An explicit 0 is honored (reconcile
# immediately — useful in tests).
# EMAIL_SENDING_STALE_MS=600000
# Wyoming STT endpoint for the dashboard's chat voice input (WARP-844).
# The orchestrator proxies browser PCM to it at POST /api/stt. Defaults to
# the wyoming-faster-whisper sidecar (linux compose profile); on macOS dev
# the sidecar doesn't run and the route answers 503 — the mic hides itself.
# STT_URL=tcp://wyoming-faster-whisper:10300
# --- Wake word (WARP-1431) ---
# Comma-separated list of wake phrases for the voice-io sidecar; the box wakes
# on ANY of them. Default wakes on both "Droplet" and "Hey Droplet". Under the
# default `vosk` engine each phrase is recognized directly with no training
# (underscores map to spaces: `hey_droplet` == "hey droplet"). Set to
# `__mock__` on a dev box with no wake runtime. Mirrors the compose default in
# docker/docker-compose.yml.
# WAKE_WORD=droplet,hey droplet
# --- Voice-pipeline sidecar image pins (WARP-590) ---
# The Wyoming wire format differs across image versions (v1 inline-data vs v2
# data_length framing), so a floating tag means a routine pull could flip the
# protocol under the STT/TTS clients. Pinned so upgrades stay deliberate; bump
# these intentionally rather than floating on `latest`. Match the compose
# defaults in docker/docker-compose.yml.
# WHISPER_IMAGE_TAG=3.1.0
# PIPER_IMAGE_TAG=2.2.2

# --- Voice turn shaping (WARP-1432) ---
# Client-side request-shape knobs that cut per-turn LLM cost for the
# always-on voice assistant. Both are OPTIONAL — leave unset for the
# curated in-code defaults (services/voice-io/voice/llm.py). Voice also
# always sends ephemeral:true (no env — it's a constant: voice has no
# persisted chat session, so a per-utterance ChatSession would just
# litter the sidebar).
# Per-turn generation cap. The box's gpt-oss voice model spends
# reasoning-channel tokens BEFORE visible content, so the default (1024)
# is deliberately generous — enough for reasoning + a short spoken
# sentence; too low empties the reply (WARP-854). Gateway hard-caps at
# 4096; an out-of-range or non-numeric value falls back to 1024.
# VOICE_MAX_TOKENS=1024
# Comma-separated tool names the voice assistant may use on tool-enabled
# turns. Empty (default) sends a curated scope — box health, cameras,
# network, files, smart devices + control_device, calendar, reminders —
# instead of the full ~43-tool set, cutting schema prefill from ~5k to
# ~1-1.5k tokens/turn. Override to tune per box; whitespace/empty
# segments are ignored and an all-empty value falls back to the default.
# VOICE_ALLOWED_TOOLS=list_cameras,get_system_health,control_device

# --- Voice VAD tuning (WARP-1434) ---
# End-of-speech detection for the STT capture window: once the user starts
# talking, the box ends the capture after a short run of trailing silence
# instead of always holding the mic for the full STT_MAX_RECORD_S. All
# OPTIONAL — leave unset for the in-code defaults (single source of truth in
# services/voice-io/voice/pipeline.py). Tune per room.
# Trailing silence (s) that ends the turn (default 0.6). Raise if the box
# cuts people off during a natural mid-sentence pause.
# VAD_SILENCE_S=0.6
# int16 frame RMS above which a frame counts as speech (default 700 — sits
# between a typical ~400 room floor and ~1000+ normal speech). This is the
# per-room knob the pipeline docstring points at: lower it in a quiet room,
# raise it in a loud one.
# VAD_SPEECH_RMS=700
# Minimum cumulative speech (s) before end-of-speech may fire (default 0.4),
# so the wake-word tail + a pause before the command don't end the turn early.
# VAD_MIN_SPEECH_S=0.4
# How long (s) the wake_detected / transcript_ready UI hints linger before
# decaying back to 'listening' so the dashboard pulse animation can play
# (default 2.0).
# WAKE_VISUAL_DECAY_S=2.0
# Query enhancement (HyDE + multi-query + adaptive routing, WARP-437) for
# search_content retrieval. Shipped dark; the RAGAS baselines it was gated
# on landed 2026-07-20, so flip to 1 per environment when rolling it out.
# Rollout steps: docs/RAG_UPGRADE_STATUS.md.
# QUERY_ENHANCEMENT_ENABLED=1
# Rate-limit trusted proxies (services/ai-gateway, GW-14). Comma-separated
# socket-peer IPs/CIDRs whose forwarded client-IP headers the rate limiter
# trusts. Empty (default) = trust nothing, always key on the real socket peer.
# Behind an nginx edge, set to nginx's container IP/subnet or every proxied
# request shares ONE bucket. Example: 172.18.0.0/16
# RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# --- BYOK Encryption (unique per device — do not share) ---
DEVICE_SECRET=change-me
# 32-byte AES key (base64) for encrypting device client secrets at rest
DEVICE_SECRET_KEY=change-me

# --- Onboarding claim (PR #373) ---
# Stable device identity shown on the Claim step's appliance card. Optional —
# defaults to a generic id if unset; the device-identity service owns the real
# serial-bound id (follow-up).
APPLIANCE_ID=
# Claim code OVERRIDE for the onboarding Claim step. The orchestrator now MINTS
# + rotates the claim code itself (WARP-632 / docs/ADR-017): on an unclaimed box
# it generates a DRPL-XXXX-XXXX code, seeds only its hash, and renders it on the
# PyPortal lid. Set CLAIM_CODE to pin a SPECIFIC code instead of minting (it's
# hashed with DEVICE_SECRET and verified by POST /api/setup/claim). Leave blank
# to let the orchestrator mint — that's the normal case for a fresh box.
CLAIM_CODE=

# --- JWT ---
JWT_SECRET=change-me

# --- Session hardening (WARP-247, NIST 800-63B) ---
# Sliding idle timeout per role class (seconds). Admin-class = owner/admin.
SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_ADMIN_SECONDS=900
SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_USER_SECONDS=3600
# Hard cap on total session lifetime from login (seconds); never extended.
SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=28800
# Max concurrent sessions per user; the oldest is evicted at login.
SESSION_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_USER=5

# --- MCP server (WARP-103) ---
# Internal port for the streamable-HTTP transport. The Compose service
# (`mcp-server`) binds this; nothing is published to the host by default.
# Bump only if you need to expose it for off-box clients (inference-engine
# running on a separate dev workstation, Claude Desktop, etc.) — see the
# `mcp-server` service in docker/docker-compose.yml for how to add a host
# port mapping.
MCP_PORT=9090
# Sentinel set by the orchestrator's mcp-client.singleton when it spawns
# the stdio child. Bypasses JWT verification on the stdio transport — the
# orchestrator process is the trusted principal. Do NOT set this in the
# global env or in the docker/docker-compose.yml `mcp-server` service env;
# only the orchestrator child should ever see MCP_TRUSTED=1.
MCP_TRUSTED=

# --- Application ---
STORAGE_BACKEND=nextcloud
AUTH_ENABLED=true
FILES_ROOT=/data/files
MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE_MB=100

# --- Device bridge (USB drives, screen QR) ---
# These are read directly via process.env (NOT in config.ts's zod schema),
# so changing them requires an orchestrator restart. Two DISTINCT vars with
# DIFFERENT defaults reach the host-side device-bridge for DIFFERENT routes:
#   BRIDGE_URL        — storage.ts reads `${BRIDGE_URL}/drives`. Default is
#                       the Linux docker0 gateway so the orchestrator (on the
#                       default bridge network) can reach the host bridge.
#   DEVICE_BRIDGE_URL — screen-qr.service.ts reads `${DEVICE_BRIDGE_URL}/openwrt/qr`.
#                       Default uses the host-gateway alias.
BRIDGE_URL=http://172.17.0.1:9090
DEVICE_BRIDGE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:9090

# --- ERP: direct-SQL Eaglesoft track (WARP-1106) ---
# Off by default, twice over: the `erp` compose profile is not in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES, and ERP_SQL_BRIDGE_URL is empty. A box that has done
# neither degrades to ERP_NOT_CONNECTED, which is the honest answer.
#
# The bridge (services/erp-sql-bridge) also needs a license-gated SAP SQL
# Anywhere client vendored into its image before it can reach anything — see
# services/erp-sql-bridge/vendor/README.md. Enabling the URL without that gets
# you a bridge that answers /health with ok:false, not a working integration.
#
# The alternative track needs none of this: `eaglesoft-api` talks to
# Patterson's official REST API over HTTPS and is configured per-connection in
# the dashboard, not here.
ERP_SQL_BRIDGE_URL=

# The practice's SQL Anywhere box, read by the BRIDGE (not the orchestrator).
ERP_DB_HOST=
ERP_DB_PORT=2638
ERP_DB_NAME=PattersonPM
ERP_DB_SERVER_NAME=PattersonPM

# Least-privilege database accounts (services/erp-connector/sql/provision.sql).
# The bridge resolves these from its own environment and picks between them by
# ROUTE (/read/* -> ro, /write/* -> rw); a caller can name the box but never
# the identity. Leave ERP_DB_RW_PASSWORD EMPTY until a write capability is
# explicitly enabled — writes are opt-in and the write account ships unusable.
ERP_DB_RO_USER=droplet_ro
ERP_DB_RO_PASSWORD=
ERP_DB_RW_USER=droplet_rw
ERP_DB_RW_PASSWORD=

# unixODBC driver name. `SQL Anywhere 17` is the SAP client; a name containing
# "postgres" switches to psqlODBC's keyword vocabulary, which is how the test
# lane (scripts/test-erp-sql-bridge.sh) drives this against a real database.
ERP_ODBC_DRIVER=SQL Anywhere 17
# `NONE` is the documented LAN default; set TLS where the server supports it.
ERP_DB_ENCRYPTION=NONE

# --- ERP: export-drop track (WARP-1964) ---
# The third transport, and the only one that needs neither a licence-gated
# driver nor vendor enrolment: the practice exports its own reports from its
# practice-management system into a folder, and Droplet reads the files.
# Provider keys are `<vendor>-export` (eaglesoft-export, dentrix-export,
# opendental-export, generic-export). Read-only by construction — an export is
# a one-way copy, so this track's applyWrite always refuses.
#
# ERP_EXPORT_DROP_HOST_PATH is the HOST path compose mounts read-only at
# /data/erp-exports — normally the mount point of a CIFS share on the practice
# LAN. ERP_EXPORT_DROP_ROOT is the CONTAINER path the orchestrator reads, and
# leaving it empty is what keeps the track off: the mount can exist while the
# track stays inert, the same opt-in-twice shape as the direct-SQL track.
#
# ERP_EXPORT_DROP_ROOT is deliberately operator config and never request input:
# a filesystem path arriving on a connect call would be an arbitrary-file read
# inside the orchestrator.
ERP_EXPORT_DROP_HOST_PATH=../data/erp-exports
ERP_EXPORT_DROP_ROOT=

# Path to a JSON file of operator-authored export profiles (header signature ->
# canonical columns). This is how an install maps a product we ship no built-in
# profile for, or corrects a built-in whose columns do not match that site's
# report layout, without waiting for a release. Read fresh on every connect, so
# fixing the file and reconnecting is enough — no restart.
# Format + worked example: docs/integrations/export-drop.md
ERP_EXPORT_DROP_PROFILES=

# --- Audit signing (WARP-456 / WARP-476) ---
# The append-only ActivityRow audit log is HMAC-SHA256 hash-chained. The
# CANONICAL key is the raw 32-byte binary file at /data/secrets/audit.key
# (AUDIT_KEY_PATH), auto-generated chmod-600 on first boot by
# scripts/lib/secrets.sh::sync_audit_signing_key and NEVER written to .env.
# AUDIT_SIGNING_KEY (base64, >=32 bytes) is the env-side key. Resolution
# order (see audit-signing.service.ts loadAuditKeyFromDisk): the disk key
# wins when it matches or when the env var is unset, BUT when both are
# present and DIFFER the behaviour splits by NODE_ENV:
#   - production            -> FATAL, refuses to boot (audit-chain fork guard).
#   - dev/CI (anything else) -> the env var OVERRIDES the disk key (logs a
#                               warn); it is NOT inert just because a disk
#                               key exists.
# So on a dev/CI box that already has /data/secrets/audit.key, a stale
# AUDIT_SIGNING_KEY silently keeps winning even after setup.sh rotates the
# disk file. Leave this unset unless the host has no /data mount, or you
# intentionally want the env var to override the disk key.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
AUDIT_SIGNING_KEY=

# --- Device intelligence (WARP-81) ---
# IEEE OUI database CSV for MAC->vendor lookups. Read via process.env;
# default resolves to <cwd>/data/oui.csv. A missing file is non-fatal —
# vendor lookups degrade to null (best-effort), not an error.
OUI_CSV_PATH=

# --- Scheduling runtime (WARP-89 / WARP-93) ---
# Read directly via process.env (not in config.ts); restart to apply.
#   SCHEDULE_TICK_MS    — firewall block/unblock diff ticker interval (MILLISECONDS).
#   DEVICE_RECONCILE_MS — DHCP + wireless + firewall snapshot poll (MILLISECONDS).
SCHEDULE_TICK_MS=30000
DEVICE_RECONCILE_MS=10000

# --- Reminders + calendar poller ---
# Single in-process interval that dispatches due reminders and re-syncs
# calendar sources. Read via process.env; value is in SECONDS (not ms).
REMINDER_POLL_INTERVAL_SEC=30

# --- Device identity (WARP-230) ---
# Selects the device-identity-svc sidecar backend.
#   real = use /dev/tpm0 via tpm2-pytss (production).
#   mock = pure-Python in-memory mock (dev / CI / hosts without a TPM).
# scripts/lib/secrets.sh sets this to 'real' on a host with /dev/tpm0
# and 'mock' otherwise. Override here if you want to force one mode.
DROPLET_TPM_BACKEND=mock
# Subject CN baked into the self-signed device identity cert. Defaults
# to `hostname`; override for fleet-wide consistency.
DROPLET_DEVICE_ID=droplet

# --- At-rest encryption (WARP-232) ---
# The LUKS2 data partition + USB drives seal their unlock keys to the SAME PCR
# set device-identity uses (DROPLET_TPM_PCRS "0,2,4,7"). systemd tools take the
# '+'-separated form; both name the identical set {0,2,4,7}. Change only in
# lockstep with DROPLET_TPM_PCRS or the seals drift onto different boot policies.
DROPLET_TPM_PCRS_BIND=0+2+4+7
# DEV ONLY. droplet-luks-provision.sh REFUSES to provision without a TPM.
# Set to 1 on a TPM-less dev box to force plain-key provisioning (the data
# partition will NOT be encrypted). Never set this on an appliance.
DROPLET_LUKS_ALLOW_NO_TPM=0

# --- Camera auto-discovery (services/camera-discovery) ---
# Subnet scanned for RTSP cameras — the camera network for the deployment
# shape (ADR-018 Decision 4). This default is the multi-box OpenWrt isolated
# camera VLAN (openwrt/files/etc/config/dhcp `cameras`). The single-box shape
# has no separate camera VLAN today — its cameras share whatever LAN the edge
# router serves, so scripts/lib/single-box.sh overrides this knob in .env to
# "auto": camera-discovery then resolves the network from the routing
# service's /network/interfaces at scan time (WARP-1805) instead of trusting
# a provision-time constant that goes stale when the fabric moves.
CAMERA_SUBNET=192.168.100.0/24
CAMERA_SCAN_INTERVAL=30
# Operator-supplied camera admin credentials, tried BEFORE the factory-default
# probe list (services/camera-discovery/default_credentials.py). Set one pair
# via the two vars below, OR a full list via CAMERA_CREDENTIALS_JSON.
# Example (single pair):
#   CAMERA_DEFAULT_USERNAME=admin
#   CAMERA_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=your-site-admin-pw
CAMERA_DEFAULT_USERNAME=
CAMERA_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=change-me
# Example (multiple pairs):
#   CAMERA_CREDENTIALS_JSON=[["admin","pw1"],["viewer","pw2"]]
CAMERA_CREDENTIALS_JSON=
# WS-Discovery multicast scan is off by default — the upstream library
# (python-ws-discovery) leaks FDs on Python 3.12+. Set to 1 to re-enable
# after pinning a patched release.
ONVIF_WS_DISCOVERY_ENABLED=0
# Auto-run the vendor first-run admin-password flow when a camera is
# detected in an uninitialized state (Hanwha Wisenet /init-cgi/pw_init.cgi
# and similar). Uses CAMERA_DEFAULT_USERNAME/PASSWORD above. Default off.
CAMERA_AUTO_INITIALIZE=0

# --- Frigate image variants ---
# Default is the CPU-only ``stable`` image. On a JetPack 6 GPU host set:
#   FRIGATE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.1-tensorrt-jp6
#   FRIGATE_RUNTIME=nvidia
# On x86_64 with a discrete NVIDIA GPU, use ``0.17.1-tensorrt`` + nvidia runtime.
FRIGATE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.1
FRIGATE_RUNTIME=runc
# Optional: trigger the JP6 image's ONNX model preparator. Leave empty
# until ``trt-model-prepare`` stops expecting legacy .cfg inputs.
YOLO_MODELS=

# --- Display (services/oled-display) ---
# Backend selection. Options:
#   auto     — probe for PyPortal on USB-serial, fall back to sim (default)
#   pyportal — force USB-serial to an Adafruit PyPortal Titano running the
#              firmware in services/oled-display/pyportal/
#   sim      — render frames to SIM_OUTPUT PNG for dev / CI
DISPLAY_BACKEND=auto
# PyPortal backend — host-side serial device path. Titano enumerates as two
# ACM endpoints; we use the second (data) one so the REPL on ACM0 stays free.
PYPORTAL_TTY=/dev/ttyACM1
PYPORTAL_BAUD=115200

# --- WARP-279: Claude-activity meta-observability dashboard ---
# Personal access token used by the orchestrator to read recent commits,
# open PRs, and CI runs from GitHub for /admin/claude-activity. Optional —
# without one, the adapter falls back to unauthenticated requests (60 req/h
# rate limit, fine for dev/sandbox). For a production-feeling experience
# point this at a fine-grained PAT scoped to the droplet-onboard-services repo
# with `metadata: read`, `actions: read`, `pull-requests: read`,
# `contents: read`. Repo override via GITHUB_REPO_OWNER / GITHUB_REPO_NAME
# (defaults: DropletByWarpLab / droplet-onboard-services).
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GITHUB_REPO_OWNER=DropletByWarpLab
GITHUB_REPO_NAME=droplet-onboard-services
# Jira credentials for the Claude-activity dashboard. All three required
# for the Jira panels (WARP-228 chain + in-flight tickets) to populate;
# missing any one degrades them to empty with an "unconfigured" banner —
# the rest of the dashboard works normally. JIRA_HOST is the bare
# atlassian.net subdomain (no scheme), e.g. `warp-lab.atlassian.net`.
JIRA_HOST=warp-lab.atlassian.net
JIRA_EMAIL=
JIRA_API_TOKEN=

# --- WARP-615: fleet-analytics agent (device -> droplet-analytics portal) ---
# Fail-open telemetry producer embedded in the orchestrator (epic WARP-614).
# OFF by default: unset/0/false keeps the analytics facade a pure no-op and
# the box behaves exactly as before. Only 1/true enables. When enabled, the
# agent also needs ANALYTICS_URL plus EITHER an ingest token (pre-provisioned)
# OR a single-use provisioning code (exchanged at /agents/register, WARP-616)
# -- otherwise it stays a no-op with one info log at boot.
ANALYTICS_ENABLED=0
# Portal base URL including the version path. Default targets a LOCAL portal
# through the host gateway; the fleet portal is
# https://analytics.warp-lab.ai/api/v1
ANALYTICS_URL=http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/v1
# SECRET bearer (dpl_<machineId>_<secret>). Never tracked, never logged.
ANALYTICS_INGEST_TOKEN=
# SECRET single-use code from the portal's /settings/tokens (WARP-616).
ANALYTICS_PROVISIONING_CODE=
# Registration tier: home | business | enterprise.
ANALYTICS_MACHINE_TIER=home
# Cadences (seconds) -- consumed by WARP-617 (buffer flush) / WARP-620
# (heartbeat); defaults sit under the portal's per-machine rate limits.
ANALYTICS_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_S=30
ANALYTICS_METRICS_FLUSH_S=60
ANALYTICS_EVENTS_FLUSH_S=5
ANALYTICS_ERROR_DEDUP_WINDOW_S=60

# --- ops-console (compose profile=ops) ---
# Bearer token the operator pastes into the ops-console UI to unlock
# /ops/*. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Distributed via 1Password / encrypted chat — never in a URL or commit.
# If unset, the container generates an ephemeral token at startup and
# logs it (NOT for production — every restart invalidates the operator's
# saved token). When the operator team grows past ~5 people OR the fleet
# past 50 units, replace with proper OIDC + per-operator audit.
OPS_TOKEN=

# ============================================================================
# Fleet SSO / OIDC (optional) — workforce sign-in via Google / Entra / Okta
# ============================================================================
# The orchestrator reads four vars PER provider (google / entra / okta). A
# provider is enabled ONLY when ALL FOUR of its vars are set; otherwise it is
# omitted from the runtime discovery endpoint (GET /api/sso/oidc/providers)
# and the dashboard shows password-only login (it fails closed — no SSO
# button that 500s mid-flow). Leave a block blank to keep that provider off.
#
# REDIRECT_URI must be the orchestrator's callback, registered verbatim at
# the IdP:  https://<your-droplet-host>/api/sso/oidc/callback
# ISSUER is the OIDC issuer URL (discovery is fetched from it):
#   google → https://accounts.google.com
#   entra  → https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
#   okta   → https://<your-org>.okta.com
# Scopes requested are fixed: "openid email profile". Secrets here are real
# OAuth client secrets — distribute via 1Password / encrypted chat, never in
# a URL or commit. See docs/ONBOARDING_SSO_OIDC.md and
# docs/ADR-016-fleet-sso-provisioning-model.md for the full setup + the
# email_verified / domain-allowlist account-linking caveats.

# --- Google Workspace ---
# DROPLET_SSO_GOOGLE_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# DROPLET_SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# DROPLET_SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DROPLET_SSO_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://<your-droplet-host>/api/sso/oidc/callback

# --- Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) ---
# DROPLET_SSO_ENTRA_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
# DROPLET_SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
# DROPLET_SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DROPLET_SSO_ENTRA_REDIRECT_URI=https://<your-droplet-host>/api/sso/oidc/callback

# --- Okta ---
# DROPLET_SSO_OKTA_ISSUER=https://<your-org>.okta.com
# DROPLET_SSO_OKTA_CLIENT_ID=
# DROPLET_SSO_OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DROPLET_SSO_OKTA_REDIRECT_URI=https://<your-droplet-host>/api/sso/oidc/callback

# ============================================================================
# Compose profiles — `COMPOSE_PROFILES` controls which optional service
# groups start on `docker compose up -d`. Comma-separated; order doesn't
# matter. Auto-set by scripts/setup.sh based on host detection; override
# here only if you know what you're doing.
# ============================================================================
#   `linux`       — Frigate (Linux-only; requires /dev/dri/renderD128 and
#                   /dev/bus/usb). Setup.sh writes this when running on
#                   Linux so Frigate starts automatically. Skipped on macOS.
#   `full`        — `switch` + `camera-discovery`. Opt-in on either OS for
#                   full hardware control. Not safe to leave on without a
#                   real switch and IP cameras on the LAN.
#   `single-box`  — `openwrt` (router-in-container) + `switch` +
#                   `camera-discovery`. Activates the single-box deployment
#                   shape (vs the multi-box shape with a separate inference
#                   host and a router host). When set, also set the single-box
#                   knobs below (FRIGATE_RENDER_NODE, OLLAMA_URL,
#                   DROPLET_TPM_BACKEND, OPENSSL_CONF, etc.) so the
#                   patched services find the right values.
#                   NOTE: this no longer carries the inference runtime — pick
#                   exactly one of `ollama` / `dmr` below.
#   `ollama`      — `ollama` (rocm) on the local dGPU. The OPT-OUT runtime,
#                   selected with `INFERENCE_RUNTIME=ollama ./scripts/setup.sh`.
#   `dmr`         — Docker Model Runner. THE DEFAULT for a fresh box (WARP-1870).
#                   `ollama` and `dmr` are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE: exactly one
#                   inference runtime may hold /dev/kfd and the render node
#                   (SINGLE GPU OWNER, WARP-1826). Two runtimes on one card is
#                   what put a 20B model on the CPU at 0/25 layers in WARP-1824.
#                   `scripts/lib/single-box.sh` appends whichever matches
#                   INFERENCE_RUNTIME and strips the other, so a setup re-run
#                   can neither start both nor leave the box with no inference.
#   `ops`         — `ops-console` (loopback :8089). Warp Lab support tunnel.
#   `docs`        — `docserver` (OnlyOffice Document Server) for in-browser
#                   Office editing + co-authoring (WARP-882). DEFAULT-ON on the
#                   32 GB box; drop on ≤8 GB boxes. The engine is OnlyOffice
#                   Community Edition (AGPLv3) and adds a ~2 GB always-on image,
#                   which a 32 GB single-box absorbs comfortably — so it IS in
#                   the example profile set below and scripts/lib/single-box.sh
#                   RAM-gates it on (above the small-box threshold) / off (on a
#                   ≤8 GB box). To turn it off manually: drop `docs` here AND set
#                   DOCS_ENABLED=0 above. With it off the dashboard renders
#                   "Edit" as unavailable, not dead.
#
# Example for the single-box deployment (without the doc-server; setup.sh adds
# `docs` when it detects ≥32 GB RAM). To enable manually on a 32 GB machine:
# append `,docs` here AND set DOCS_ENABLED=1 above, and ensure
# ONLYOFFICE_JWT_SECRET is set via `openssl rand -hex 32`.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=linux,single-box,dmr

# ============================================================================
# Single-box deployment knobs — only meaningful when `single-box` is in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES.
# ============================================================================
# Most of these are no-ops in the multi-box deployment shape (defaults match
# what the multi-box inference-host + router-host + separate-switch layout expects).
# Setup.sh auto-writes the right values when it detects single-box hardware
# (dGPU + iGPU present + no separate inference host on the LAN).

# Frigate render node. Default `/dev/dri/renderD128` works on a single-GPU host
# On the single-box shape, the AMD dGPU is at renderD128
# (reserved for Ollama) and the iGPU is at renderD129 (used by Frigate).
# Override here only if your hardware enumerates differently.
FRIGATE_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128

# Ollama render node — same idea but for the bundled `ollama` service.
# Default targets the first AMD render device. Override if Ollama needs
# a different GPU than Frigate.
OLLAMA_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128

# WireGuard listen port published by the bundled `openwrt` container.
# Customer-facing; punch this through the upstream router as port forward.
WIREGUARD_PORT=51820

# ── FIPS 140-3 mode (per-customer option, default OFF) ──────────────────────
# WARP-318. The single knob. `0` (default) → modern crypto (TLS 1.3, OpenSSL
# defaults), identical to a non-FIPS install. `1` → every service boots
# FIPS-enforcing against the NIST-validated OpenSSL FIPS provider (CMVP #4282,
# WARP-967) already baked DORMANT into every image — NO rebuild needed. Flip
# with `scripts/setup.sh --fips` (or `--no-fips`); setup.sh derives
# OPENSSL_CONF / DROPLET_FIPS_REQUIRED / NODE_OPTIONS from this value. Do NOT
# set those by hand — setup.sh manages them atomically.
# See docs/fips.md. FIPS RESTRICTS algorithms for certification; it does not
# add strength.
DROPLET_FIPS_MODE=0

# The vars below are DERIVED from DROPLET_FIPS_MODE by `setup.sh --fips`
# and are managed by setup.sh — do NOT edit them here. Listed (empty = OFF)
# only so operators recognize them. When DROPLET_FIPS_MODE=1 setup.sh writes:
#   OPENSSL_CONF=/etc/ssl/openssl-fips.cnf   DROPLET_FIPS_REQUIRED=true
#   NODE_OPTIONS=--openssl-shared-config
# (NODE_OPTIONS is Node-only; Python uses the system libcrypto so
# OPENSSL_CONF alone activates it.)
#
# NEVER add OPENSSL_MODULES here (WARP-1063): a process-wide module path
# forces every libcrypto instance in a multi-OpenSSL process (Node + Prisma,
# pyca cryptography + system libssl) onto ONE fips.so, which the second
# initializer cannot activate (openssl#25553) — that service then dies with
# P1011 / LIBRARY_HAS_NO_CIPHERS at TLS-client construction. Each runtime
# instead resolves its own baked-in module copy shipped in the image; an
# empty value is NOT harmless either (it becomes a real, empty search path).
# setup.sh actively removes the key. See docs/fips.md.
OPENSSL_CONF=
DROPLET_FIPS_REQUIRED=

# Routing service → in-container OpenWrt. On the single-box shape the
# routing container talks to the bundled `openwrt` container via
# 127.0.0.1:8181 (openwrt publishes that port on host loopback). On the
# multi-box shape, the routing container talks to the real router host
# at OPENWRT_HOST. Setup.sh writes the right values automatically based on
# detected deployment shape.
#   single-box:  OPENWRT_HOST=127.0.0.1, OPENWRT_PORT=8181, OPENWRT_USERNAME=root
#   multi-box:   OPENWRT_HOST=192.168.50.1, OPENWRT_PORT=80, OPENWRT_USERNAME=droplet-ai
#   edge-router: OPENWRT_HOST=192.168.9.1, OPENWRT_PORT=80, OPENWRT_USERNAME=droplet-ai
#
# The `edge-router` shape is the DROPLET-MINI two-chip split: routing runs on a
# separate Pi 5 running OpenWrt (repo: DropletByWarpLab/droplet-edge-router),
# and this box sits on its LAN side. The bundled `openwrt` container is NOT the
# router in that shape — point OPENWRT_HOST at the Pi's LAN address.
#
# OPENWRT_PASSWORD for the edge-router shape is the value the Pi generated at
# first boot into /etc/droplet/droplet-ai-password. Copy it into
# docker/secrets/openwrt_password (the secret file wins over the env var; see
# WARP-37). There is no automatic pairing handshake yet — after any Pi reflash
# (`sysupgrade -n` regenerates every per-unit credential) re-copy the password
# and re-run ./scripts/setup.sh --sync-secrets; until then the Network tab
# shows the typed "Credentials rejected" state (WARP-1673).
#
# The edge-router shape's COMPANION DEVICES speak the same rpcd/ubus +
# droplet-ai protocol (ADR-033):
#   switch: SWITCH_DRIVER=openwrt, SWITCH_HOST=192.168.9.2, SWITCH_PORT=80,
#           SWITCH_USERNAME=droplet-ai, SWITCH_PASSWORD= the switch's own
#           /etc/droplet/droplet-ai-password (→ docker/secrets/switch_password)
#   AP:     AP_OPENWRT_PASSWORD below (→ docker/secrets/ap_openwrt_password);
#           the AP's address comes from mDNS discovery, not env.
# WARP-815: when left blank, docker-compose falls back to the single documented
# default 192.168.50.1:80 / droplet-ai (the multi-box router; matches
# services/routing/main.py + CLAUDE.md + services/routing/README.md).
OPENWRT_HOST=
OPENWRT_PORT=
OPENWRT_USERNAME=

# Router-hardware escape hatches (WARP-1845) — rarely needed; both resolve
# correctly on their own. WAN_DEVICE_NAME pins the WAN kernel netdev for the
# traffic sampler; empty = resolve live from `network.interface.wan status`
# (Pi edge router: eth1, MikroTik RB5009: p1 — never hardcode these).
# DROPLET_MGMT_INTERFACES is the comma-separated set of interfaces the
# interface-write routes refuse to touch without `force`. Leave EMPTY to keep
# the shipped default (lan,mgmt) — compose deliberately falls back to that
# real default, because an empty value would disable the guard entirely.
WAN_DEVICE_NAME=
DROPLET_MGMT_INTERFACES=

# --- External OpenWrt AP credential (WARP-1675, ADR-033) ---
# The rpcd password of an EXTERNAL Droplet OpenWrt AP (droplet-edge-router
# ap/ image, e.g. the Zyxel NWA50BE). The AP mints its own per-unit
# droplet-ai password at first boot — copy /etc/droplet/droplet-ai-password
# off the AP here; setup.sh --sync-secrets materializes it to
# docker/secrets/ap_openwrt_password (the secret file is what the routing
# container reads). Blank = no external AP: coverage-extender approvals keep
# the historical router-side-only behaviour. AP_USERNAME / AP_PORT only need
# setting if the AP image diverges from droplet-ai / 80.
AP_OPENWRT_PASSWORD=

# WARP-815: Wi-Fi scan radio for /wireless/scan + /wireless/clients. Blank →
# the SDK falls back to the literal `wlan0` (multi-box UCI radio). On the
# single-box, setup.sh writes the AP radio `wlp14s0` (sourced from
# DROPLET_AP_IFACE) so the scan targets the radio the box actually has.
DROPLET_WIFI_SCAN_DEVICE=

# --- Fleet telemetry agent (WARP-963) ---
# Outbound-only, fail-open push of operational telemetry to the Warp Lab
# analytics portal (droplet-analytics agent API). DOUBLE-gated and OFF by
# default: the container only starts when `telemetry` is in COMPOSE_PROFILES,
# and the process only dials out when DROPLET_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=1 AND
# credentials exist (a one-time provisioning code below, or the identity a
# previous registration persisted on the fleet-agent-state volume). Payloads
# are operational shape only — no file names, no user data, no LAN client
# detail. Full egress audit table: services/fleet-agent/README.md.
DROPLET_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=0
# Agent-API base URL (the portal's /api/v1 root).
DROPLET_TELEMETRY_PORTAL_URL=https://analytics.warp-lab.ai/api/v1
# One-time registration code minted by an operator in the portal's
# /settings/tokens. Consumed on first successful register; the returned
# ingest token lives ONLY on the fleet-agent-state volume (never tracked,
# never in .env). Leave empty until an operator provisions this box.
DROPLET_TELEMETRY_PROVISIONING_CODE=
# Optional operator-pinned location for the portal fleet map (WARP-433).
# Country = ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 (e.g. US, DE); region = free-form 1-100 chars.
# Invalid or empty values are OMITTED from heartbeats entirely (an empty
# geoRegion would 422 the whole heartbeat server-side).
DROPLET_TELEMETRY_GEO_COUNTRY=
DROPLET_TELEMETRY_GEO_REGION=
# Reported to the portal in X-Droplet-FW + register/inventory payloads.
DROPLET_FIRMWARE_VERSION=
# Registration tier: home | business | enterprise.
DROPLET_TIER=home
# Operator-facing name this box registers under. RegisterRequest.hostname is
# captured ONCE into the portal's Machine.hostname (unless the operator
# pre-set a hostname on the provisioning code), so set it BEFORE first
# registration. Empty → the fleet-agent container's pinned hostname
# ("droplet" by default — see docker-compose.yml), never Docker's random
# container hex.
DROPLET_HOSTNAME=

# --- Restic backups (WARP-254) ---
# Repository location for the scheduled restic backups (daily incremental,
# weekly full, monthly restore drill — see scripts/host/README.md). Blank →
# the default local path on the data disk, /var/lib/droplet/restic-repo.
# The repository password is DERIVED from DEVICE_SECRET_KEY at run time
# (HKDF-SHA256) and is never stored anywhere. Off-device targets (sftp/S3/
# rest-server, which restic speaks natively) are future work — this knob is
# the only thing that will need to change.
DROPLET_BACKUP_TARGET=
# Camera footage (the nvrdata NVR volume) is EXCLUDED from backups by default:
# 24/7 recordings routinely run to hundreds of GB, dwarfing every other
# surface and stretching the nightly window from minutes to hours. Set to 1
# to opt in.
DROPLET_BACKUP_INCLUDE_CAMERA=

# --- Remote-access TLS issuance (WARP-978 / ADR-023) ---
# HQ_ISSUANCE_URL: base URL of the fleet-HQ issuance API — a PUBLIC (non-secret)
# Cloudflare Worker. Safe to commit; this is the default baked into setup.sh so a
# plain reflash / --single-box self-provisions its trusted droplet-us.com cert
# zero-touch (no SSH-in to hand-set it). Overridable at provisioning time by
# exporting HQ_ISSUANCE_URL before running setup.sh. Plain outbound HTTPS — no WG
# tunnel required. NOTE: TUNNEL_TOKEN (the Cloudflare Tunnel connector token) is a
# SECRET and is deliberately NOT defaulted here — it stays empty unless provisioned.
# This is the LIVE HQ Worker URL — the vanity name fleet-hq.droplet-us.com is not
# yet bound in DNS (NXDOMAIN as of 2026-07-16); a box pointed at it dead-ends
# issuance at DNS resolution and silently stays self-signed. Flip this default to
# the vanity name in the SAME commit that creates its DNS binding at the HQ
# production cutover (droplet-fleet-hq docs/HQ-CUTOVER-RUNBOOK.md).
HQ_ISSUANCE_URL=https://droplet-fleet-hq.rjouffret.workers.dev
